<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tausret</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1188 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tawosret, Twosret, Thuoris, Sitre Meryamun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tausret, also spelled Tawosret or Twosret, was the last known ruler of the Nineteenth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Probably the second royal wife of Seti II, and not a king's daughter, she became regent with Chancellor Bay for his heir Siptah after Seti's death; Bay was executed on Siptah's orders in his Year 5, a move Aidan Dodson believes Tausret engineered. When Siptah died she took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, counting his nearly six years as her own; inscriptions of her Year 8 on her mortuary temple at Gournah suggest perhaps three years of sole rule. Her reign ended in a civil war, most likely with her overthrow by Setnakhte, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>, who usurped her tomb KV14.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tausret.jpg" alt="Tausret" width="330" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scan by NYPL · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tausret.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tausret's birth date is unknown. She did not hold the title of King's Daughter, so her later claim to kingship came only through her marriage to Seti II. No children of the couple are known, unless tomb KV56 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a>, where Theodore Davis found jewellery bearing their names, holds the burial of a child. Aldred thought it the tomb of a daughter, while Maspero thought the objects belonged to Tausret's own burial. After Seti II's death she acted as regent for his heir Siptah, probably her stepson, together with the chancellor Bay, a man of West Asian origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> ostracon published by Pierre Grandet records that Bay was killed on the order of Siptah in the fifth year of that king, on the 27th day of the third month of Shemu. Aidan Dodson believes the initiative was Tausret's, so that she no longer had to share power with her rival. When Siptah died she took the throne herself, with the title Daughter of Re, and counted his years as her own, which explains why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> gives her seven years as Thuoris. Papyrus Harris I claims that during these years Egyptian territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> fell effectively under a man called Irsu, who allied himself with her.</p><p>Her highest known date, year 8, comes from a foundation block of her mortuary temple at Gournah, found in 2011 by the University of Arizona Egyptian Expedition. Since the temple was largely completed, with walls, plaster and a roof, she probably ruled for a ninth year, which would give her about three years of sole rule. Her name appears at Abydos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a>, Memphis and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. A pair statue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> shows Siptah seated on her lap, and her name is linked with the turquoise mines at Serabit el Khadim and Timna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Her reign ended in a civil war, described on the Elephantine stela of Setnakhte, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>. Most likely he overthrew her, though it is not known whether she was deposed or died in office. Setnakhte took over her tomb KV14, replacing her images with his own, and reburied Seti II in KV15. Her mortuary temple was dismantled and its blocks were reused at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-habu">Medinet Habu</a> early in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>, whose list of kings there leaves out Amenmesse, Siptah and Tausret. Her mummy has not been found; some scholars think the woman known as Unknown Woman D, from KV35, may be Tausret, but the only support is that the mummification fits the period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tausret” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The throne usually passed from father to son, but Hatshepsut and <b>Tausret</b> ruled as kings in their own right.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
